<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salihiyya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Salihiyya order</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Salihiyya is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in the late 19th century by Muhammad Salih, a disciple in the Ahmadiyya tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-idris">Ahmad ibn Idris</a>. It spread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, where its strict teaching, including a ban on tobacco and qat, won followers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-abdullah-hassan">Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</a> joined it in Mecca and spread it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, where it became the religious basis of his Dervish movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salihiyya.jpg" alt="Salihiyya" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Douglas James Jardine (1888-1946) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammed_Abdullah_Hassan-dj.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
